select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-024","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Rank LPG, diesel, furnace oil and bagasse by calorific value. Which is lowest?","back":"Bagasse is lowest (roughly 2,200–2,300 kcal/kg as fired, because of about 50% moisture). LPG is highest (~11,000+ kcal/kg). Diesel and furnace oil are about 10,000–10,800 kcal/kg.","explain":"Biomass fuels such as bagasse and rice husk have low calorific value because of moisture and ash. They still matter as low-cost renewable fuels for sugar mills and cogeneration.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q11","order":1124},
{"id":"p1-025","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What two measurements are needed to find the relative humidity of air with a psychrometer?","back":"Dry Bulb Temperature (DBT) and Wet Bulb Temperature (WBT). RH is then read from a psychrometric chart. A sling psychrometer measures both.","explain":"DBT indicates the sensible heat content of the air-water vapour mixture. The wet-bulb depression (DBT − WBT) shows how dry the air is. When the depression is zero, no evaporation is possible and RH = 100%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q14, Q21, Q32","order":1125},
{"id":"p1-026","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What happens to the relative humidity when air is heated without changing its moisture content (absolute humidity)?","back":"RH decreases, because warmer air can hold more moisture at saturation while the actual moisture stays the same.","explain":"This is the principle of pre-heating air before a dryer: it lowers RH and increases the air's capacity to pick up moisture. Cooling air at constant moisture raises RH until it reaches 100% at the dew point.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q26","order":1126},
{"id":"p1-027","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Define power factor. What is the PF of a circuit with resistive and inductive load?","back":"PF = kW ÷ kVA = cos φ. A resistive + inductive (R-L) circuit has a lagging PF of less than 1. Also, kVA² = kW² + kVAr².","explain":"Inductive loads such as motors and transformers draw reactive power (kVAr) for their magnetic fields. That raises kVA and current for the same kW, which increases I²R losses and kVA-based demand charges. Capacitors supply kVAr locally to improve PF. Only a pure resistance gives unity PF.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q24","order":1127},
{"id":"p1-028","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"An induction motor nameplate says 30 kW, 85% efficiency. How much power does it draw at full load?","back":"35.29 kW. The nameplate kW is shaft output, so input = 30 ÷ 0.85 = 35.29 kW.","explain":"Motor ratings always state mechanical output. Electrical input = output ÷ efficiency. Audits compare measured input kW with rated input to estimate percentage loading.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q30","order":1128},
{"id":"p1-029","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How does wind turbine power vary with wind speed, and what is the formula?","back":"P = ½ ρ A V³, so power varies with the cube of wind speed: tripling the speed gives 27× the power.","explain":"For example, ρ = 1.2 kg/m³, A = 200 m² and V = 8 m/s gives 0.5 × 1.2 × 200 × 512 ≈ 61.4 kW available in the wind. A turbine captures only part of this, with the Betz limit at 59.3%. Yaw control turns the rotor to face the wind, and pitch control adjusts the blade angle.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q16, Q25; BEE 2025 P1 L3","order":1129},
{"id":"p1-030","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Roughly what voltage does a single silicon PV cell produce, and what is its working principle?","back":"About 0.5 V. It works by the photovoltaic effect: photons create electron-hole pairs at a p-n junction, which produces DC current.","explain":"Cells are connected in series and parallel in modules to reach usable voltage and power. Cell efficiency = maximum power output ÷ (insolation × cell area), so it does not depend on the inverter. An inverter converts DC to AC.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q20, Q22; BEE 2025 P1 L3","order":1130},
{"id":"p1-031","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What are the reactants and by-product in a hydrogen fuel cell?","back":"Hydrogen combines electrochemically with oxygen to generate electricity, and water is the by-product (plus heat).","explain":"A fuel cell converts chemical energy directly into electricity without combustion, so it is not limited by Carnot efficiency. Hydrogen is oxidised at the anode, and oxygen is reduced at the cathode.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q35","order":1131},
{"id":"p1-032","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the composition of producer gas, and how is gasifier efficiency calculated?","back":"Producer gas is mainly CO, H₂ and CH₄ (combustible), diluted with N₂ and CO₂. Gasifier efficiency = (gas volume × gas CV) ÷ (biomass mass × biomass CV).","explain":"Example: 1 kg of wood at 4,000 kcal/kg giving 2 m³ of gas at 1,000 kcal/m³ → 2,000/4,000 = 50% efficiency. Producer gas has a low CV (about 1,000–1,200 kcal/Nm³) because it contains a lot of nitrogen from the air.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q21, Q49","order":1132},
{"id":"p1-033","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Solar radiation reaching the earth consists of which bands?","back":"Ultraviolet, visible and infrared radiation.","explain":"Roughly half of solar energy is in the infrared and most of the rest is visible light. A small part is ultraviolet, most of which is absorbed by stratospheric ozone.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q25","order":1133},
{"id":"p1-034","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A plant uses in one year: 12 million kWh of grid electricity; 1,500 kL of furnace oil (density 0.93 kg/L, GCV 10,200 kcal/kg); and 8,000 t of coal (GCV 4,000 kcal/kg). It produces 20,000 t of product. Find the total annual energy use in toe and the specific energy consumption in toe/t. Take 1 kWh = 860 kcal.","back":"Total ≈ 5,655 toe per year; SEC ≈ 0.283 toe/t of product.","explain":"Converting every energy source to toe through kcal is exactly how a plant checks whether it qualifies as a Designated Consumer, and how SEC is reported under PAT. Coal is the biggest share here, about 57% of the total.","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert electricity to toe.","answer":"12×10⁶ kWh × 860 = 1.032×10¹⁰ kcal → ÷10⁷ = 1,032 toe","explain":"1 kWh = 860 kcal; 1 toe = 10⁷ kcal."},{"prompt":"Convert furnace oil to toe.","answer":"1,500 kL × 1,000 L × 0.93 = 13,95,000 kg; × 10,200 = 1.4229×10¹⁰ kcal → 1,422.9 toe","explain":"Mass = volume × density; energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Convert coal to toe.","answer":"8,000 t = 8×10⁶ kg × 4,000 = 3.2×10¹⁰ kcal → 3,200 toe","explain":"Energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Add up and compute SEC.","answer":"Total = 1,032 + 1,422.9 + 3,200 = 5,654.9 toe; SEC = 5,654.9 ÷ 20,000 = 0.283 toe/t","explain":"SEC = total energy ÷ production."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L4 (modified)","order":1134},
{"id":"p1-b03-001","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish potential energy and kinetic energy, and give two forms of each.","back":"Potential energy is stored energy due to position or configuration (forms: chemical, nuclear, stored mechanical, gravitational). Kinetic energy is energy of motion (forms: radiant, thermal, motion, sound, electrical).","explain":"Examples: a raised pile-driver head has potential energy of position; a compressed spring has potential energy of configuration. A moving vehicle, flowing fluid or moving machine parts have kinetic energy.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p83","order":1135},
{"id":"p1-b03-002","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Give the formulas for gravitational potential energy, kinetic energy and nuclear (mass-energy) energy.","back":"PE = m g h; KE = ½ m v²; E = m c² (c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s).","explain":"In fission, a uranium nucleus splits and loses a little mass, which is released as energy according to Einstein's equation. Because c² is so large, a tiny mass loss gives an enormous amount of energy.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p83","order":1136},
{"id":"p1-b03-003","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which is an example of stored mechanical energy: water in a reservoir, an arrow in a stretched bow, an airborne aeroplane, or you on a mountain top?","back":"An arrow in a stretched bow. Stored mechanical energy is energy stored in objects by applying a force, like compressed springs or stretched rubber bands.","explain":"Water in a reservoir and a person on a mountain are examples of gravitational (position) energy. An airborne aeroplane has both kinetic and gravitational energy. This is a frequent MCQ.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p84","order":1137},
{"id":"p1-b03-004","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How do radiant energy and sound energy differ in the way they travel?","back":"Radiant energy is electromagnetic energy travelling in transverse waves (light, X-rays, gamma rays, radio waves; solar energy is an example). Sound travels through substances as longitudinal (compression/rarefaction) waves.","explain":"Radiant energy needs no medium, so it crosses the vacuum of space from the sun. Sound needs a material medium. Thermal energy is the vibration and movement of atoms and molecules, for example geothermal energy.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p84","order":1138},
{"id":"p1-b03-005","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Define the joule and the watt, and give the formula for the power of a rotating shaft.","back":"1 J = work done when a 1 N force moves through 1 m in its direction (1 N·m). 1 W = 1 J/s. For a rotating shaft, P = T ω = 2πTN/60 (T in N·m, N in rpm).","explain":"Power is the rate of doing work: P = W/t, so energy W = P t. For large quantities the units are MJ (10⁶ J), GJ (10⁹ J), TJ (10¹² J) and PJ (10¹⁵ J). Work done by a rotating body is W = T·θ, with θ in radians.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p85","order":1139},
{"id":"p1-b03-006","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A portable machine needs a force of 200 N to move it. How much work is done moving it 20 m, and what average power is used if this takes 25 s? (Example 3.1)","back":"Work = 4,000 J (4 kJ); power = 160 W.","explain":"Work = force × distance in the direction of the force; power = work ÷ time. 1 J/s = 1 W.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p85","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the work formula.","answer":"W = F × s","explain":"Work done by a constant force along its line of action."},{"prompt":"Compute the work.","answer":"200 N × 20 m = 4,000 N·m = 4 kJ","explain":"1 N·m = 1 J."},{"prompt":"Compute the power.","answer":"P = W/t = 4,000 J ÷ 25 s = 160 W","explain":"Average power over the movement."}],"order":1140},
{"id":"p1-b03-007","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How many times per second does the current reverse direction in a 50 Hz AC supply?","back":"100 times per second (twice every cycle).","explain":"DC is unidirectional and non-varying, like a battery's output. AC reverses at regular intervals. The normal utility supply frequency in India is 50 Hz.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p86","order":1141},
{"id":"p1-b03-008","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"An electric heater consumes 1.8 MJ when connected to a 250 V supply for 30 minutes. Find its power rating and the current it draws. (Example 3.2)","back":"Power = 1,000 W (1 kW); current = 4 A.","explain":"Power = energy ÷ time (time in seconds). Then use P = V I for the current.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p87","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert time to seconds.","answer":"30 min × 60 = 1,800 s","explain":"SI units: J and s give W."},{"prompt":"Compute the power.","answer":"P = 1.8 × 10⁶ J ÷ 1,800 s = 1,000 W = 1 kW","explain":"Power = energy/time."},{"prompt":"Compute the current.","answer":"I = P/V = 1,000 ÷ 250 = 4 A","explain":"From P = V I."}],"order":1142},
{"id":"p1-b03-009","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 100 W bulb is connected to a 250 V supply. Find (a) the current and (b) the resistance of the bulb. (Example 3.3)","back":"(a) 0.4 A; (b) 625 Ω.","explain":"Use P = V I for current, then Ohm's law R = V/I. The book repeats the same problem as Example 3.5, with the unit misprinted there as W instead of Ω.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p88","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the current.","answer":"I = P/V = 100 ÷ 250 = 0.4 A","explain":"From P = V I."},{"prompt":"Find the resistance.","answer":"R = V/I = 250 ÷ 0.4 = 625 Ω","explain":"Ohm's law."},{"prompt":"Check using P = V²/R.","answer":"250² ÷ 625 = 100 W ✓","explain":"Consistent with the rating."}],"order":1143},
{"id":"p1-b03-010","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"An electric kettle has a resistance of 30 Ω. What current flows on a 240 V supply, and what is its power rating? (Example 3.4)","back":"Current = 8 A; power = 1,920 W (1.92 kW).","explain":"Ohm's law gives the current, and P = V I gives the power. You could also use P = V²/R = 240²/30 = 1,920 W.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p88","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the current.","answer":"I = V/R = 240 ÷ 30 = 8 A","explain":"Ohm's law."},{"prompt":"Find the power.","answer":"P = V I = 240 × 8 = 1,920 W","explain":"Electrical power."},{"prompt":"State the rating.","answer":"1.92 kW","explain":"Divide by 1,000."}],"order":1144},
{"id":"p1-b03-011","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 230 V, 5 kW electric heater is used for hot water. Find the electricity consumed per hour (a) at rated voltage, (b) at 200 V. (Example 3.6)","back":"(a) 5 kWh; (b) about 3.78 kWh.","explain":"A resistive heater's power varies with the square of voltage (P = V²/R), so P at 200 V = 5 × (200/230)² = 3.78 kW. The book's printed working shows (200/230) × 5 without the square, but its answer of 3.78 kWh is the squared result.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p88","steps":[{"prompt":"Consumption at rated voltage.","answer":"5 kW × 1 h = 5 kWh","explain":"Energy = power × time."},{"prompt":"State how heater power varies with voltage.","answer":"P = V²/R, so P ∝ V² (resistance constant)","explain":"The heater is a fixed resistance."},{"prompt":"Compute power at 200 V.","answer":"5 × (200/230)² = 5 × 0.756 = 3.78 kW","explain":"Ratio of voltages squared."},{"prompt":"Consumption per hour at 200 V.","answer":"3.78 kW × 1 h ≈ 3.78 kWh","explain":"Low voltage also means less heat output, so heating takes longer."}],"order":1145},
{"id":"p1-b03-012","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Write the power-triangle relationships between kW, kVA, kVAr and power factor.","back":"kW = kVA cos φ; kVA = kW / cos φ; kVAr = kVA sin φ; PF = cos φ; also kW = √(kVA² − kVAr²).","explain":"Active (resistive) power does useful work. Reactive power builds the magnetic flux of inductive devices but is otherwise not usable. Apparent power is the hypotenuse. Only components in the same phase can be added, kW to kW and kVAr to kVAr. In an MCQ, all three expressions for active power are correct ('all of the above').","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p89","order":1146},
{"id":"p1-b03-013","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What usually causes current to lag voltage in an AC system?","back":"Inductive load, for example motors and transformers, which need reactive current to build their magnetic fields.","explain":"A purely resistive load keeps current in phase with voltage, while a capacitive load makes current lead. Lagging reactive power (kVAr) is what lowers the power factor of typical industrial loads.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p89","order":1147},
{"id":"p1-b03-014","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Give the power formulas for balanced three-phase and single-phase loads.","back":"Three-phase: P = √3 × V_L × I_L × cos φ. Single-phase: P = V × I × cos φ (V_L = line voltage, I_L = line current).","explain":"In industry, single-phase power is mostly used for lighting, fractional-HP motors and electric heaters. Larger motors and process loads are three-phase.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p89","order":1148},
{"id":"p1-b03-015","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 400 W mercury vapour lamp runs 10 h/day on 230 V, drawing 2 A at PF 0.8. Find the energy consumed per day. (Example 3.7)","back":"About 3.7 kWh per day.","explain":"For a single-phase AC load, kW = V × I × cos φ / 1,000. Multiply by hours for kWh. The measured input (0.368 kW) is a little below the nameplate 400 W.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p90","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the single-phase energy formula.","answer":"kWh = V (kV) × I × cos φ × hours","explain":"Single-phase AC power includes power factor."},{"prompt":"Compute the input power.","answer":"0.230 × 2 × 0.8 = 0.368 kW","explain":"Voltage expressed in kV."},{"prompt":"Compute the daily energy.","answer":"0.368 × 10 = 3.68 ≈ 3.7 kWh","explain":"Energy = power × time."}],"order":1149},
{"id":"p1-b03-016","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How do you convert a motor's horsepower rating to input kVA?","back":"kVA = (HP × 0.746) / (η × PF), where η is motor efficiency and PF is motor power factor.","explain":"HP × 0.746 gives output kW. Dividing by efficiency gives input kW, and dividing by PF gives kVA. Smaller motors running at part load are the least efficient and have the lowest power factor.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p90","order":1150},
{"id":"p1-b03-017","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Does a motor's nameplate kW/HP rating show input or output? What about its nameplate volts, amps and PF?","back":"Nameplate kW/HP is the output (shaft) power at full load. Nameplate volts, amps and PF describe input conditions at full load.","explain":"This is why rated input kW is calculated as √3 × V × I × PF from nameplate values, and the rated efficiency is output kW ÷ that input. Mixing up the two is a common exam trap.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p90","order":1151},
{"id":"p1-b03-018","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 3-phase induction motor (20 kW) driving a pump is measured at 440 V, 25 A, PF 0.90. Find its energy consumption in one hour. (Example 3.8)","back":"About 17.15 kWh.","explain":"Measured three-phase input power = √3 × V × I × cos φ. It is input power, so it is compared with rated input (not the 20 kW output rating) when judging loading.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p90","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the three-phase power formula.","answer":"P = √3 × V_L × I_L × cos φ","explain":"Balanced three-phase load."},{"prompt":"Compute the power.","answer":"1.732 × 0.440 kV × 25 A × 0.90 = 17.15 kW","explain":"Voltage in kV gives kW."},{"prompt":"Compute one hour's energy.","answer":"17.15 kW × 1 h = 17.15 kWh","explain":"Energy = power × time."}],"order":1152},
{"id":"p1-b03-019","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 3-phase, 10 kW motor has nameplate 415 V, 18.2 A, PF 0.9. Running, it measures 415 V, 12 A, PF 0.7. Find rated input, rated efficiency, actual input power and percentage loading. (Example 3.9)","back":"Rated input ≈ 11.8 kW; efficiency ≈ 85%; actual input ≈ 6.0 kW; loading ≈ 51%.","explain":"Motor loading % = measured input kW ÷ rated input kW × 100. Rated input comes from nameplate V, I and PF because those are full-load input conditions. The 10 kW rating is output.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p91","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute rated input at full load.","answer":"1.732 × 0.415 × 18.2 × 0.9 = 11.8 kW","explain":"Nameplate V, I, PF are input conditions."},{"prompt":"Compute rated efficiency.","answer":"10 ÷ 11.8 ≈ 85%","explain":"Rated output ÷ rated input."},{"prompt":"Compute actual input.","answer":"1.732 × 0.415 × 12 × 0.7 = 6.0 kW","explain":"Measured values."},{"prompt":"Compute percentage loading.","answer":"6.0 ÷ 11.8 × 100 ≈ 51% (book: 51.2%)","explain":"Loading = measured input ÷ rated input."}],"order":1153},
{"id":"p1-b03-020","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 10 kW motor has a full-load efficiency of 85%. At a particular load it measures 415 V, 10 A, PF 0.68 (3-phase). Find the motor loading in percent. (Chapter question S-2)","back":"About 41.6% loading.","explain":"When nameplate V/I/PF are not given, find rated input from rated output ÷ efficiency. Then loading = measured input ÷ rated input.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p105","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute rated input.","answer":"10 kW ÷ 0.85 = 11.76 kW","explain":"Nameplate kW is output; input = output/η."},{"prompt":"Compute measured input.","answer":"1.732 × 0.415 × 10 × 0.68 = 4.89 kW","explain":"Three-phase power formula."},{"prompt":"Compute loading.","answer":"4.89 ÷ 11.76 × 100 ≈ 41.6%","explain":"Loading = measured input ÷ rated input."}],"order":1154},
{"id":"p1-b03-021","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A 250 W sodium vapour street lamp runs 12 h/day on 230 V, drawing 2 A at PF 0.85. Find the energy consumption per day. (Chapter question S-3)","back":"About 4.69 kWh per day.","explain":"Single-phase: kW = V × I × cos φ. Measured input includes ballast losses, which is why it exceeds the 250 W lamp rating.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p106","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute the input power.","answer":"0.230 kV × 2 A × 0.85 = 0.391 kW","explain":"Single-phase power with PF."},{"prompt":"Compute the daily energy.","answer":"0.391 × 12 = 4.69 kWh","explain":"Energy = power × hours."},{"prompt":"Compare with the lamp rating.","answer":"0.391 kW > 0.25 kW lamp rating","explain":"The difference is mostly control-gear (ballast) loss."}],"order":1155},
{"id":"p1-b03-022","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Give the conversions between °C, °F and K.","back":"°F = °C × 1.8 + 32; °C = (°F − 32) / 1.8; K = °C + 273.15 (≈ +273).","explain":"Water freezes at 32 °F and boils at 212 °F at atmospheric pressure. The Kelvin scale has the same 1° step as Celsius but starts at absolute zero (−273.15 °C), and gas-law calculations require absolute temperature in K.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p91","order":1156},
{"id":"p1-b03-023","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Why must absolute pressure, not gauge pressure, be used in gas-law calculations, and at what conditions is standard atmospheric pressure defined?","back":"Gas laws relate absolute quantities, so pressure must be absolute (measured from vacuum) and temperature in kelvin. Standard atmospheric pressure is defined at sea level at 273 K (0 °C): 1.01325 bar = 101,325 Pa.","explain":"Gauges read the difference from the surrounding atmosphere, and atmospheric pressure varies with temperature and altitude. 20 °C (293 K) is also used as a reference temperature.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p92","order":1157},
{"id":"p1-b03-024","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Define the calorie and give its value in joules.","back":"The heat needed to raise 1 g of water by 1 °C. 1 cal = 4.187 J (≈ 4.2 J); 1 kcal raises 1 kg of water by 1 °C.","explain":"The joule is now the internationally accepted unit of heat. The kcal is still widely used in Indian fuel and boiler practice, for example in GCV figures in kcal/kg.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p93","order":1158},
{"id":"p1-b03-025","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How many specific heats does a gas have, and why do solids and liquids have just one?","back":"Gases could have infinitely many (one per process), but two are defined: c_p (constant pressure) and c_v (constant volume). For solids and liquids, specific heat does not depend on the process.","explain":"Specific heat is the heat needed to raise 1 kg by 1 °C (1 K), in kcal/kg °C or J/kg K, and it varies with temperature. Gases expand significantly when heated, so the heat needed depends on whether pressure or volume is held constant.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p93","order":1159},
{"id":"p1-b03-026","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"From Table 3.1, give the specific heats (J/kg °C) of lead, copper, iron, aluminium, alcohol and water.","back":"Lead 130, mercury 140, copper 390, iron 470, aluminium 910, alcohol 2,400, water 4,200.","explain":"Lead has the lowest value and water by far the highest. That is why water absorbs or releases a large amount of heat for a small temperature change, which makes it an excellent heat-transfer and storage medium.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p93","order":1160},
{"id":"p1-b03-027","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What are the latent heat of fusion of ice and the latent heat of vaporisation of water at 100 °C?","back":"Fusion: 335 kJ/kg. Vaporisation: 2,257 kJ/kg (≈ 540 kcal/kg).","explain":"Latent heat changes state without changing temperature. Q = m × h_fg (or h_if for fusion). The same amount is released on reversal: about 2,257–2,260 kJ/kg when steam condenses at 100 °C, and 335 kJ/kg when water freezes.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p94","order":1161},
{"id":"p1-b03-028","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"If the latent heat of fusion of water is 335 kJ/kg, how much latent heat is given up when 10 kg of water at 0 °C freezes to ice at 0 °C? (Example 3.10)","back":"3,350 kJ.","explain":"Freezing releases the same latent heat that melting absorbs, at the fusion temperature and with no change in temperature.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p94","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the latent heat formula.","answer":"Q = m × h_if","explain":"Phase change at constant temperature."},{"prompt":"Substitute the values.","answer":"Q = 10 kg × 335 kJ/kg","explain":"Mass and latent heat of fusion."},{"prompt":"Compute.","answer":"Q = 3,350 kJ (released)","explain":"Heat leaves the water as it freezes."}],"order":1162},
{"id":"p1-b03-029","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"20 kJ of heat is supplied to 25 kg of ice at 0 °C. How many kg of ice melt? (Example 3.11)","back":"About 0.06 kg.","explain":"Only the heat supplied limits how much melts. The 25 kg is the amount available, not the amount melted.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p94","steps":[{"prompt":"Rearrange the latent heat formula.","answer":"m = Q / h_if","explain":"Mass melted by a given heat input."},{"prompt":"Substitute the values.","answer":"m = 20 kJ ÷ 335 kJ/kg","explain":"Latent heat of fusion = 335 kJ/kg."},{"prompt":"Compute.","answer":"m ≈ 0.06 kg (59.7 g)","explain":"Far less than the 25 kg of ice present."}],"order":1163},
{"id":"p1-b03-030","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"Find the heat needed to vaporise 2 m³ of water at 100 °C if the latent heat of vaporisation is 2,257 kJ/kg. (Example 3.12)","back":"4,514,000 kJ (≈ 4.51 GJ).","explain":"Convert volume to mass (1 m³ of water ≈ 1,000 kg), then Q = m × h_fg.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p95","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert volume to mass.","answer":"2 m³ × 1,000 kg/m³ = 2,000 kg","explain":"Density of water ≈ 1,000 kg/m³."},{"prompt":"Apply the latent heat formula.","answer":"Q = 2,000 × 2,257","explain":"Q = m × h_fg."},{"prompt":"Compute.","answer":"Q = 4,514,000 kJ ≈ 4.51 GJ","explain":"Only latent heat, since the water is already at 100 °C."}],"order":1164},
{"id":"p1-b03-031","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Why is steam superheated in power plants, and how does saturation temperature change with pressure?","back":"Superheating (heating steam above saturation temperature at the existing pressure) improves efficiency and avoids condensation in the turbine. The higher the pressure, the higher the saturation temperature.","explain":"On the T-S diagram, the area under the dome is the wet water-steam mixture and constant-pressure lines there are horizontal (latent heat region). The region to the right of the x = 1.0 (dry saturated) line is superheated steam.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p95","order":1165},
{"id":"p1-b03-032","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Define dew point and specific humidity (humidity ratio).","back":"Dew point is the temperature at which the water vapour in air becomes saturated and starts condensing, equal to the saturation temperature at the vapour's partial pressure. Specific humidity is kg of water vapour per kg of dry air.","explain":"Relative humidity, in contrast, is the ratio of the vapour actually held to the vapour the air could hold if saturated at the same temperature, expressed as a percentage. Air at 50% RH holds half the moisture it could hold at that temperature.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p96","order":1166},
{"id":"p1-b03-033","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"When are dry-bulb, wet-bulb and dew-point temperatures all equal?","back":"When relative humidity is 100% (saturated air).","explain":"In unsaturated air, evaporation from the wet wick cools the wet bulb below the dry bulb, and the dew point is lower still. At saturation no evaporation occurs, so all three readings coincide.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 03 p96","order":1167}
]$cards$::jsonb);
